title: 'Era points for blocks validators author on system chains'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `sp-staking` gains `PointsKind`, the service for which validators earn reward points:
    `Validation` (relay chain validation) and `Collation` (blocks authored on system chains),
    with pinned codec indices.

    `pallet-staking-async` gains `reward_service_points(kind, points)`, a public function that
    credits points earned for a service other than relay chain validation to the active era and
    emits `ServicePointsCredited`. Accounts not exposed in the active era are dropped.
    `PointsKind::Validation` is refused, since those points arrive only with the relay chain
    session report. `ErasRewardPoints` stays the combined tally of every service, so points from
    system chains also move the self-stake incentive share and count as activity for
    `chill_inactive`. A new storage item, `ErasRewardPointsByKind`, records the same points per
    `PointsKind`. It is written with the tally, pruned with it and checked by try-state against
    it. No migration. The new `WeightInfo::reward_service_points` must be added by external
    implementors of the trait.

    `pallet-validator-collators` counts the blocks each author produces as a
    `pallet_authorship::EventHandler`. At a report it hands the counts of the authors in the
    stored validator set to the runtime's `BlockReporter`, in chunks of at most
    `MaxEntriesPerReport` entries, inside one storage layer. If the reporter rejects a chunk, every
    count is kept for the next report. A report runs every `ReportPeriod` blocks, and once per
    set `ReportMargin` before the expected era end: the arrival time of the set, recorded in
    `SetReceivedAt` from `UnixTime`, plus `ExpectedEraDuration`. The margin used is at
    most `MaxReportMargin` and at most half of `ExpectedEraDuration`. The early report is not retried: if the reporter
    rejects it, its counts go out with the next periodic report. Governance sets the period with
    `set_report_period`, the expected era duration with `set_expected_era_duration` and the
    margin with `set_report_margin`, up to `MaxReportMargin`. New required config items:
    `BlockReporter`, `DefaultReportPeriod`, `MaxEntriesPerReport`, `UnixTime`,
    `DefaultExpectedEraDuration`, `DefaultReportMargin`, `MaxReportMargin`. The runtime adds the
    pallet to the `EventHandler` of `pallet-authorship`.

    Adds `pallet-staking-async-system-chain-client`, which runs on Asset Hub only and receives
    the counts. Other chains call `report_authored_blocks` from `ReportOrigin`, which names the
    reporting chain, and the local chain credits its own counts through `credit_authored_blocks`.
    A count becomes `count × PointsPerUnit[(source, Collation)]` era points, handed to the
    runtime's `RewardPoints` as `Collation` points. `PointsPerUnit` is keyed by source chain and
    `PointsKind`. A source without points per unit earns nothing. Governance sets the values
    with `set_points_per_unit`, up to `MaxPointsPerUnit`, and cannot weight `Validation`. The
    client weighs a report by its number of entries, and each reporting chain bounds the size
    of what it sends.

    People Westend reports its counts to Asset Hub Westend over XCM. Asset Hub Westend adds
    `pallet-staking-async-system-chain-client` and credits its own counts locally.
    People sends at most `MaxEntriesPerReport` entries per report, and Asset Hub hands its own
    counts over in one chunk.
